#!/bin/sh
# koti-help — the manual, on the machine.
#
# ⛔ WHY THIS EXISTS RATHER THAN JUST docs/MANUAL.md: koti cannot fetch it.
# A machine that cannot fetch anything needs its documentation locally, or the
# documentation is only useful to someone sitting at a different computer. This
# is the short form; docs/MANUAL.md is the long one and they are kept in step
# by hand -- if you add a section there, add its heading here.
#
# Copyright (c) 2026 Joonatan Alanampa
# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

exec more <<'EOF'
koti - a computer built from the transistor up.
RV32IMA + Zicsr, sv32 MMU, 32 MB SDRAM, Linux 6.12, on an ULX3S 85F at 25 MHz.

WHAT IS HERE
  Userspace is busybox on musl, statically linked: about 280 commands. The
  usual ones behave the way you expect -- ls cd cp mv rm mkdir cat less more
  grep sed awk find head tail sort wc du df mount umount ps top kill free
  uname dmesg date vi.

  vi is the editor. `less` and `more` page. `tree` is here. `bc` and `dc` do
  arithmetic. `tar`, `gzip`, `xz`, `unzip` handle archives.

STORAGE
  /            RAM (initramfs). ANYTHING WRITTEN HERE IS LOST AT POWER-OFF.
  /dev/kotisd2 the microSD's second partition, ext2, and it is writable:
                   mount /dev/kotisd2 /mnt
                   ... work ...
                   sync ; umount /mnt
  ⚠️ ext2 has NO JOURNAL. `sync` before pulling the power or you will lose
  whatever was still in the page cache.

THE TWO CONSOLES
  The monitor (tty1) is the one you type at. The serial line (hvc0) is a
  MIRROR: it receives the same keystrokes, so a single login logs you in on
  both and every command runs twice. That is deliberate -- the serial echo is
  how the machine is debugged -- but it is why you may see doubled output.

KEYBOARD
  Finnish layout, loaded at boot (`koti: fi keymap loaded`). AltGr reaches
  @ $ { } [ ] \ | -- all of which a shell needs.

INTERNET
  koti has no address of its own -- no MAC, no PHY -- so ip, ping and wget
  exist as busybox applets and still fail. What it has instead is a modem:
  the ESP32 next to the FPGA, reached over /dev/ttyKOTI0.
    koti-net wake                     release the ESP32 from reset
    koti-net join <ssid> <password>   prints the address it was given
    koti-net get http://example.com/  the page, on standard output
    koti-net off                      put it back into reset
  The address belongs to the ESP32, not to koti. http only, no https.
  Waking it puts a second driver on the microSD bus (the ESP32's GPIOs ARE
  the card's wires) -- measured harmless over 25 runs, but `koti-net off`
  when you are done is the right habit.

WHAT IS NOT HERE
  NO package manager, no compiler, no floating point (koti has no FPU).

SHUTTING DOWN
  sync, then pull the power. `poweroff` and `halt` stop the CPU (the firmware
  halts on the SBI reset call); there is no power controller to switch off.

MORE
  docs/MANUAL.md in the repository, which this summarises.
EOF
